<DocsLayout title="FAQ and troubleshooting" description="Clear answers to common RedstoneLimiter configuration questions: regions, 0 limits, sliding windows, GUI, whitelist, quarantines and permissions.">
<div class="page-head"><div class="eyebrow">HELP / NO GUESSWORK</div><h1 class="page-title">Questions, answered.</h1><p class="lead">Common questions from server administrators, including the settings that are easy to misunderstand.</p></div>
<div class="prose">
<details class="details" open><summary>What does “regions.enabled: true” actually do?</summary><p>It activates an aggregate activity limit for each <strong>32×32-chunk region</strong>. All reached activity within that large area contributes to the region's period count. The base maximum is <code>regions.max-activations</code> (default 96), and preset/TPS multipliers may raise it. This is not the same as a single chunk limit.</p></details>
<details class="details"><summary>I only want to stop big machines that lag one area. What settings should I start with?</summary><p>Start with <code>blocks</code> and <code>subchunks</code> to target an individual device or compact machine. Inspect stats with <code>/rl</code> and <code>/rl chunk</code>, then adjust <code>chunks</code> if many devices across a chunk add up. Use <code>regions</code> deliberately because a single region covers 1,024 chunks. The <a href="/examples/">first example</a> demonstrates a local-only approach.</p></details>
<details class="details"><summary>Does “max-activations: 5” mean only five uses forever?</summary><p>No. It means five allowed recorded attempts inside the configured <code>duration-millis</code> activity window, before preset and TPS multipliers. A sixth attempt exceeds the effective threshold when it remains 5. Older activity eventually drops out or the fixed period resets.</p></details>
<details class="details"><summary>What is the difference between duration and sustain?</summary><p><code>duration-millis</code> measures how long the plugin counts recent activity. <code>sustain-millis</code> is the temporary block duration after the count goes over the limit. Continued excessive attempts extend the temporary block. Neither is a permanent quarantine.</p></details>
<details class="details"><summary>Does disabling a scope turn all redstone protection off?</summary><p>No. It stops that particular scope from threshold-blocking, but still records its telemetry. Other enabled scopes, manual quarantine and independent piston push-size checks can still block activity. Disabling a whole world using <code>disabled-worlds</code> is different: it bypasses the plugin's counting/blocking/notification pipeline for that world.</p></details>
<details class="details"><summary>What does setting a max to 0 mean?</summary><p>Zero means <strong>unlimited for that specific scope or material threshold</strong>. For example, <code>PISTON: 0</code> removes the individual-piston activity maximum, but other scopes and <code>max_piston_push</code> can still block piston activity. Oversized piston extensions are cancelled before the automatic-limit whitelist check. This does not mean “always block”.</p></details>
<details class="details"><summary>Why is a piston blocked even though its configured max hasn't been reached?</summary><p>A wider scope (subchunk, chunk, or region) might have hit its limit, a prior over-limit attempt may still be under sustain, the piston may push more than <code>max_piston_push</code> blocks, or the chunk may be manually quarantined. Check TPS scaling and the preset before comparing raw counts to the base max.</p></details>
<details class="details"><summary>Does the plugin automatically quarantine laggy chunks?</summary><p>No. Automatic limits temporarily block the offending activity scope. Quarantine is <strong>manual only</strong>, stays until removed, and overrides whitelist entries.</p></details>
<details class="details"><summary>What is sliding=true vs false?</summary><p>With <code>true</code>, the count represents the recent rolling duration (using time buckets). With <code>false</code>, counting uses a fixed period that resets when its duration elapses. A previous sustain deadline may continue after the reset.</p></details>
<details class="details"><summary>Why does STRICT allow more activations than my YAML number?</summary><p>STRICT uses a 1× <em>preset</em> multiplier. The separate TPS multiplier is enabled by default and can reach 2× at or above 19.5 TPS. For example, a base maximum of 3 can become 6. Turn TPS scaling off only if you want fixed effective maxima.</p></details>
<details class="details"><summary>Does RedstoneLimiter limit mobs, entities or hoppers moving items?</summary><p>Its core purpose is observable <strong>redstone/circuit events</strong>, not generic entity limiting. The <code>HOPPER</code> material threshold concerns observed redstone-related activity, not every inventory item transfer or a universal hopper-item throughput limit.</p></details>
<details class="details"><summary>How do I get notifications and find a bad machine?</summary><p>Allow <code>redstonelimiter.notify</code> (or your customized <code>notifications.permission</code>), confirm <code>notifications.enabled: true</code>, and use <code>/rl notifications</code>. View aggregate stats with <code>/rl</code> and the control panel with <code>/rl gui</code>.</p></details>
<details class="details"><summary>Can I change everything in the GUI?</summary><p>The GUI supports scope limits, per-material maxima, presets, whitelist and quarantine policies. Some advanced settings such as Redis networking and notification cache sizing are documented in <a href="/configuration/">config.yml</a> instead.</p></details>
<details class="details"><summary>Is RedstoneLimiter compatible with Folia?</summary><p>The uploaded plugin descriptor declares <code>folia-supported: true</code>. Actual runtime compatibility should still be validated on the exact server build you operate. The word “region” in this plugin's thresholds is a fixed 32×32-chunk geometric area, not a Folia scheduler region.</p></details>
<details class="details"><summary>I edited config.yml but the changes don't appear. What now?</summary><p>Check indentation (YAML uses spaces), ensure you changed the appropriate scope and material key, save the file and run <code>/rl reload</code> with permission. Consider the effects of preset/TPS scaling and broader scopes. If your server uses a migrated config, compare with the <a href="/config.yml">current defaults</a>.</p></details>
